======================================================================
STATISTICAL ANALYSIS REPORT
======================================================================

--- PRIMARY METRIC: Projection Gap (ΔP) ---
ΔP = 109.6568   (paired bootstrap, 10000 resamples, n = 96 pairs)
95% CI (percentile): [91.0948, 128.5876]
95% CI (BCa)       : [91.2710, 128.7440]   <- bias/skew corrected
Interval EXCLUDES zero -> the gap is significant
Bootstrap two-sided p: 0.00010
ΔP is a direct measurement, not a fitted classifier, so it is not
subject to the probe's overfitting critique.

--- PAIRED T-TEST ---
t = 11.5097
p = 0.000000
Cohen's d = 1.1747
Significant (α=0.01): True
Interpretation: LARGE effect: refusal direction is STRONGLY reduced in agent format

--- PERMUTATION TEST ---
Observed ΔP = 109.6568
Empirical p = 0.000000
Null 95% CI: [-28.6151, 28.7610]
Significant: True

--- BENIGN CONTROL (difference-in-differences) ---
ΔP harmful (headline): +109.6568
ΔP benign  (control) : +103.3383   p = 0.000000
Interaction          : +6.3185   p = 0.530500
Share of the harmful shift also seen on benign prompts: +94.2%
Only the interaction is specific to refusal. The rest is what
moving from chat to agent does to EVERY prompt.

--- AUROC BY FORMAT ---
                    held-out   in-sample
AUROC (chat)          0.8954      0.9132
AUROC (agent)         0.8995      0.9329
AUROC gap            -0.0041     -0.0197
Held-out = each prompt scored by a direction fitted without it.
In-sample = the direction was fitted on these same prompts; that
column is a fit statistic, not a detection rate.

======================================================================
CONCLUSION: the projection shifts between formats, but NOT more
for harmful prompts than for benign ones (interaction p = 0.5305).
→ The agentic format DISPLACES the representation; it does not
  selectively weaken refusal. Do not report ΔP as loss of safety.
======================================================================

